00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:48.720
leader, and they leave with 12 opinions

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:50.640
but no decisions. Real leaders are

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:51.720
confident, but they decide what matters.

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:53.520
They're going to force you to have a

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:55.560
conversation about it, and then tell

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:56.880
everyone what [music] happens next. I

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:58.960
have a belief that in leadership,

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:01.000
whoever controls the frame controls the

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:02.760
debate. Now, this isn't about dominating

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:04.360
people. It's about refusing to let the

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:06.040
first person who speaks [music] define

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:07.800
the problem fully. Someone says, "We

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:09.280
need to hire three more people." A weak

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:11.240
leader starts debating head count and

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:13.320
budgets. A strong leader says, "We were

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:15.240
actually talking about a problem with

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:17.240
capacity. We're not sure yet that the

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:19.280
solution to capacity is a head count

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:21.160
problem. We have four team priorities.

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:22.960
Nobody knows which three matter. Before

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:25.120
we add people, we've actually got to

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:26.760
make sure that that is the real solution

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:29.160
to this problem." So, the first frame

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:31.000
asks, "Can we afford more people?" The

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:32.680
better frame asks, "Why does the current

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:34.520
team have too much low-value work? Why

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:36.160
is there a capacity restraint?" [music]

00:10:34.520 --> 00:10:37.840
You do not want to fight inside somebody

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:39.920
else's frame. This is a great lesson for

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:41.360
life. So, here's what you want to say

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:42.920
instead. "I think that's a separate

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:44.839
problem. Let's back channel that for a

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:46.600
second, or let's hold that to the side.

00:10:44.839 --> 00:10:47.920
This is the real problem today. This is

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:49.960
first principles." And you're going to

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:52.080
drag the room back to what actually

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:54.440
matters. Next, when you communicate as a

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:55.920
leader, I call it speaking in headlines.

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:58.120
Speaking in headlines, start with the

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:59.560
point, not the preamble. So, have you

00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:01.200
guys ever experienced this? Like, you're

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:02.600
in a meeting, people give you six

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:04.680
minutes of background, three

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:06.600
disclaimers, two apologies before they

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:08.360
even talk about anything. That is a

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:10.000
nightmare. There's nothing worse as an

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:12.760
employee than having a poor leader as a

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:15.440
communicator that rambles. This is how

00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:16.480
we get taken hostage by our own company.

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:18.600
That is how your employees feel

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:20.600
sometimes, too. So, stop the apology,

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:22.320
kill the backstory, just say the damn

00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:24.200
thing. I I call this cutting words,

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:25.137
adding weight. So, a weak version might

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:25.240
be something like,

00:11:25.137 --> 00:11:26.400
>> [music]

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:28.520
>> "I've been looking through the reports,

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:30.200
and I'm not totally sure, but maybe our

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:31.800
onboarding is causing insurance, so

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:32.920
maybe we should talk to more customers,

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:35.280
and we should figure out the solution to

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:36.927
the problem. Instead, a strong version

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:37.000
cut words add weight is

00:11:36.927 --> 00:11:38.840
>> [music]

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:40.640
>> churn increase this month. Maya is going

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:42.920
to interview 10 canceled customers by

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:46.040
Thursday, present the top three causes

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:47.320
Friday, and recommend a fix. The first

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:49.080
asks the room for, I don't know,

00:11:47.320 --> 00:11:51.120
emotional permission, and the second

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:53.560
gives it a priority and an owner and a

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:55.240
deadline, and we like those. I think the

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:57.160
other thing that most owners do that

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:59.120
they don't realize or most bad leaders

00:11:57.160 --> 00:12:01.080
do that they don't realize is they talk

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:02.840
too fast. They rush through some of

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:04.720
this. Instead of cutting words and

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:07.280
adding weight, they're they're sounding

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:08.960
anxious, not authoritative. And so, we

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:12.000
call this being a gravity-focused

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:14.040
leader. So, stop machine-gunning words

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:15.880
like the meeting room is on fire. Make

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:18.040
the point, then shut up. Don't be long

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:20.080
and wit long-winded just cuz you can,

00:12:18.040 --> 00:12:22.200
but take a pause. Most people destroy

00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:24.120
their strongest sentence by panicking

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:25.920
and adding five weaker ones. You know,

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:28.680
you might say like, "We all know we're

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:30.000
avoiding the real problem." Pause. The

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:32.440
first person who rushes to fill the

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:35.320
silence, that's usually exactly where

00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:37.839
the problem is. So, after being really

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:40.200
grave and non-anxious, I want you to

00:12:37.839 --> 00:12:41.839
also think about calm. So, typically,

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:43.480
who do you follow in a crisis? Do you

00:12:41.839 --> 00:12:45.400
follow the one that's like, "Fire!"

00:12:43.480 --> 00:12:47.320
while I start running around? No. You

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:49.160
follow the person whose nervous system

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:50.440
isn't actually [ __ ] the bed.

00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:52.320
People trust leaders who seem

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:54.760
unshakable. I like to think about this

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:56.183
as like, your job as a leader is to not

00:12:54.760 --> 00:12:56.240
look like you're freaking out

00:12:56.183 --> 00:12:57.760
>> [music]

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:59.440
>> while you're actually freaking out

00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:01.280
because everybody else is freaking out

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:03.280
around you, but you know your one job as

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:05.560
a leader is not to freak out. So, your

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:07.480
job [music] is to seem unshakable.

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:09.560
Certainty is contagious. If you're

00:13:07.480 --> 00:13:11.280
panicking, that's going to spread. If

00:13:09.560 --> 00:13:12.559
you're calm, that's going to spread. So,

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:14.240
choose which one you want multiplying

00:13:12.559 --> 00:13:16.320
[music] through the company or through

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:18.400
your teams. I think, you know, the line

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:19.760
that people hate that I get so much crap

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:21.200
for on the internet, you guys can tell

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:23.040
me what you think, there is no crying in

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:25.200
business. Do not cry in meetings when

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:27.920
things are going sideways. Do not show

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:29.880
that type of emotional non-regulation.

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:31.400
It's not that you can't cry at all. Go

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:33.960
home and cry in the shower like an

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:35.200
adult. Cry later. Vent privately. When

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:37.040
the building [music] is burning though,

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:39.600
your team doesn't need to watch the fire

00:13:37.040 --> 00:13:41.360
chief collapse, right? That sucks. I'm

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:43.560
sorry. That's the job of leader. Now, if

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:45.120
you're an employee and you don't aspire

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:46.720
to leadership, cry all you want. Totally

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:48.840
fine. But nobody's going to put you in

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:50.600
charge of hard things if you're crying

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:52.520
all the time. And this is not popular

00:13:50.600 --> 00:13:54.480
and people will lie to you on the

00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:57.120
internet about this. They will lie to

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:59.400
you. You will make less money if you

00:13:57.120 --> 00:14:00.920
cry. Period in business, end of story,

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:02.320
not going to happen. It's not okay. It's

00:14:00.920 --> 00:14:03.840
not you letting your feelings out and

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:05.440
because you're more empathetic. Maybe

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:07.160
you are all those things. It doesn't

00:14:05.440 --> 00:14:08.680
[ __ ] matter. I hate to tell you this,

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:11.080
but I wish that somebody would tell me

00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:12.680
the unpopular truths, which is calm

00:14:11.080 --> 00:14:13.800
doesn't mean you don't feel it and

00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:15.800
authority doesn't mean that you're

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:17.600
screaming at people. It means I'm

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:19.520
emotionally regulating myself right now.

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:21.200
I'm not going to push all of my emotions

00:14:19.520 --> 00:14:23.560
onto somebody else. I'm going to stay

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:25.400
useful when everyone else is losing

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:28.040
their [ __ ] Next, I want you to obsess

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:29.560
on one word, action. Like every

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:31.240
important conversation that you have

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:33.440
should end with three things, a

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:35.240
decision, an owner, and a date.

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:36.640
Actually, my chief of staff Azad is

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:38.040
incredible at this. I left a meeting

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:39.920
yesterday without getting that, which is

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:41.080
a little bit unlike me, but uh

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:42.000
you're going to do this, too. And he

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:43.640
literally goes, "Wait a second. Can

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:45.280
everybody please stay? Cody, you know,

00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:47.520
you can leave, but who is in charge of

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:49.200
these? What are the deliverables? And

00:14:47.520 --> 00:14:51.120
what date are they due by?" If it ends

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:52.240
with, "Great job, everybody." Well, god,

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:53.440
you're wasting their time. Your

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:55.000
employees don't need somebody to waste

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:56.480
their time. They need somebody who's

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:58.320
going to help them get a clear action so

00:14:56.480 --> 00:15:00.560
they can actually work less on the

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:02.560
things that they want more to get better

00:15:00.560 --> 00:15:04.320
and get paid more for it. So, always try

00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:05.800
to finish with a clear action. Influence

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:07.680
without direction, that's just you being

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:09.400
noisy and doing a power play. So, don't

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:10.720
leave it open-ended. Say, "I think the

00:15:09.400 --> 00:15:12.960
summary here is we're choosing option

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:14.800
two, which means Sarah's going to own

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:16.280
it. The first version is due by Thursday

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:18.160
at 3:00, and we review it Friday

00:15:16.280 --> 00:15:19.440
morning. Is that right, everybody?" Yes,

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:21.120
no, maybe so. Then you put it in

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:22.920
writing. You put it in your to-do list.

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:24.920
There's no mystery. There's no plausible

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:26.560
deniability. And no one thought, "Ah,

00:15:24.920 --> 00:15:29.240
somebody else had it." This is how you

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:31.600
lead from below. You also stop wasting

00:15:29.240 --> 00:15:33.600
and dumping unsorted problems onto your

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:35.800
boss like a wet bag of garbage. I know

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:36.880
employees, bosses suck. Plenty of them

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:38.200
are terrible. It's really hard to be a

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:40.000
boss, so maybe give your boss a little

00:15:38.200 --> 00:15:42.000
bit of credit. Takes way more than

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:43.400
anybody thinks to actually be able to

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:44.600
lead a full team. And by the way, when

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:46.080
you're in charge, people don't listen to

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:47.400
you all the time, even if you pay them.

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:49.080
It's a very funny [music] thing. So, if

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:50.600
you want to actually get paid more and

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:52.560
become a better boss or if you want to

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:54.040
run your own things one day, then when

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:55.520
you bring them a problem, bring them the

00:15:54.040 --> 00:15:57.080
options, [music] your recommendation,

00:15:55.520 --> 00:15:59.160
and the risk of following it. So, don't

00:15:57.080 --> 00:16:00.280
say, "Huge problem. What do we do?" Say,

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:01.640
"Here's what I think we should do.

00:16:00.280 --> 00:16:03.320
Here's why. Here's what's happening."

00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:04.720
That's how you earn [music] authority

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:05.960
before you have to be the one that

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:07.480
actually pays for payroll. Let's talk

00:16:05.960 --> 00:16:09.760
about ownership really quick. When

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:11.360
you're wrong, don't hide behind, "I

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:12.720
don't know. I don't know who did that."

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:14.200
Just say, "I made the call. I

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:16.080
underestimated the risk. We're going to

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:18.080
change the plan. Sarah owns the

00:16:16.080 --> 00:16:19.560
correction, but I own the review of it

00:16:18.080 --> 00:16:21.360
by Friday. That's going to be on me

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:23.160
whether that that falls or not. You're

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:24.560
never going to be a good leader without

00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:26.400
accepting ultimate [music] ownership.

00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:27.920
And actually, a a friend of ours, Jocko

00:16:26.400 --> 00:16:29.720
Willink, has an entire book about

00:16:27.920 --> 00:16:33.000
extreme ownership. Even [music] when a

00:16:29.720 --> 00:16:34.956
problem is not of your creation, own it.

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:36.600
Be like, "That is my fault. You're

00:16:34.956 --> 00:16:38.440
[music] right. Yeah, I wasn't the one

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:40.280
that wrote the script that was bad.

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:41.600
Yeah, I wasn't the one that didn't have

00:16:40.280 --> 00:16:43.440
this fully outlined. [music]

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:44.600
Oh yeah, they did tell me this." No, no,

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:45.880
don't tell me any of that. That tells me

00:16:44.600 --> 00:16:47.080
you're not really a leader, you're a

00:16:45.880 --> 00:16:49.200
manager. You know what you should do

00:16:47.080 --> 00:16:50.839
instead? Shoot, I told you this and it

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:51.920
was that. [music] That is on me. I'll do

00:16:50.839 --> 00:16:53.440
everything I can to not have that happen

00:16:51.920 --> 00:16:55.680
again. People will forgive your bad

00:16:53.440 --> 00:16:57.400
decisions. What they stop forgiving is

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:58.480
watching a leader bend reality to

00:16:57.400 --> 00:16:59.600
protect their ego. By the way, they

00:16:58.480 --> 00:17:00.920
already know what's going on anyway.

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:02.760
Most leaders who have gotten there after

00:17:00.920 --> 00:17:04.480
a while pretty smart. And this floor, it

00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:05.680
does still have a ceiling. You can only

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:07.360
communicate so well with the wrong

00:17:05.680 --> 00:17:08.959
people. So, sometimes the problem isn't

00:17:07.360 --> 00:17:10.280
your voice or you taking ownership, it's

00:17:08.959 --> 00:17:12.199
the other people in the room. And so,

00:17:10.280 --> 00:17:13.439
floor three is how you hire A players

00:17:12.199 --> 00:17:15.360
who don't just understand the standard,

00:17:13.439 --> 00:17:17.000
they raise it. So, floor three is all

00:17:15.360 --> 00:17:18.920
about hiring people who raise the

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:21.400
ceiling for everybody. So, in this

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:23.560
instance, I like to think about four

00:17:21.400 --> 00:17:25.520
sections. Let's start with define. If we

00:17:23.560 --> 00:17:26.800
want to find other A-players to do

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:29.920
things with us, whether these are

00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:32.360
co-founders, colleagues, people to rely

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:34.960
on, it starts with defining even what an

00:17:32.360 --> 00:17:38.040
A-player is. So, the best people are

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:39.800
obsessed with the result. You know, it's

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:41.280
just really hard to beat somebody who is

00:17:39.800 --> 00:17:43.240
doing the thing that you call work and

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:45.640
they call fun. So, [music] if they're

00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:47.200
not obsessed with the game, one, that

00:17:45.640 --> 00:17:49.320
probably means that they don't aren't

00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:52.240
able to see the levels of the game.

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:54.400
Like, business, whether I'm selling

00:17:52.240 --> 00:17:56.320
laundromats, whether I'm selling

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:58.320
baseballs, whether I'm talking to you on

00:17:56.320 --> 00:18:00.000
YouTube, is all the same game. And so, a

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:01.720
lot of times, if you don't like

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:03.640
something, it's because you don't

00:18:01.720 --> 00:18:05.560
actually realize the game you're playing

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:06.920
and what it's leading to next. So, it

00:18:05.560 --> 00:18:08.960
doesn't mean you're burnt out. It

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:11.800
actually means exceptional outcomes

00:18:08.960 --> 00:18:14.120
require periods of intense or maybe

00:18:11.800 --> 00:18:15.680
exceptional intensity. A-player is not

00:18:14.120 --> 00:18:18.000
someone with a polished resume who

00:18:15.680 --> 00:18:19.120
interviews well. Here's what it is. It's

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:21.040
they have a history of winning. They

00:18:19.120 --> 00:18:22.640
create momentum without permission. And

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:24.440
they raise the performance of everyone

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:26.080
around them. We call these bar raisers.

00:18:24.440 --> 00:18:27.480
So, they actually walk into casts and

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:29.240
they kind of like it. They bring some

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:32.160
recommendations when there are problems,

